# shir_nodes/take_lines.node — expression-level semantic IR node.
# Take first/last N lines or bytes (lowered from `head`/`tail`).
#
# Backends: Perl lines().take(), JS split().slice(), C fgets, Rust lines()
# Fallback: sh2.takeLines(text, count, from_end, bytes)

node TakeLines
tag "TakeLines"
kind expr
# storage_class str_bounded
field text: expr
field count: expr
field from_end: bool
field bytes: bool
spec: none (unstated: the split-on-LF-keeping-empty-fields / take-N / rejoin-with-LF
# reading is pinned, and it needs NO list inverse. Model it at the byte level as
# `count_lf` (LF count) plus `cut n s` = "cut at the Nth LF and drop that LF", with
# `nlines s = 1 + count_lf s`. That yields the truncation law (count_lf s < n gives
# cut n s = s, otherwise s = cut n s @ LF # r), the count law (0 < n gives
# nlines (cut n s) = min n (nlines s)), empty input is one empty line, and CR/CRLF/NUL
# are ordinary bytes. What is missing is only the Isar plumbing, not the semantics:
# `cut`'s recursion needs `if k = 0` on its budget, and every attempted proof shape
# (cases on the schematic count; cases on the ⋀-bound count; induction on the count
# with the text arbitrary plus `split: list.split if_split`) left the branch goal
# unreduced, while ground instances evaluate fine. No law is approximated here.)
